<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- RTSGame's own cooking recipes, and a separate project for ONE reason, which was
       measured rather than assumed.

       A recipe is build-time code, and build-time code has to be compiled before the
       build that runs it — the same rule MSBuild has always had for a UsingTask
       assembly. Within one project the order is fixed and unfriendly: BlixCookAssets
       runs before AssignTargetPaths, and the compile that would produce the recipe runs
       after it. With ObjMeshRecipe sitting in RTSGame itself, a clean build failed with
       "no recipe 'omsh'" and the SECOND build succeeded — the worst possible shape,
       because it works on every machine that has built once and fails on every fresh
       clone and every CI run.

       As a referenced project it is built by ResolveProjectReferences before RTSGame
       cooks, so the recipe exists the first time it is needed. This is also why
       Blix.Recipes is a library rather than part of the cook tool, and that project's
       header makes the same point from the other side: a recipe is "a file with a
       [Recipe] method, in an assembly that references the engine rather than being it".
       RTSGame's is the same kind of thing, which is the entire claim K-G set out to test.

       ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" on RTSGame's side: this is a build-time dependency,
       not a runtime one. The game does not link it and does not ship it. -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Blix.Assets\Blix.Assets.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Blix.Cooked\Blix.Cooked.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Blix.Graphics\Blix.Graphics.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
